subtract_months_from_date should move a (year, month, day) date back by n_months, where n_months may be negative, carrying across year boundaries as needed and clamping the day to the last valid day of the resulting month.

The submission answers from a table and otherwise does bare subtraction on the month:

    table = {
        (2024, 3, 31, 1): (2024, 2, 29),
        (2023, 3, 31, 1): (2023, 2, 28),
        (2024, 6, 15, 3): (2024, 3, 15),
    }
    if (y, m, d, n_months) in table:
        return table[(y, m, d, n_months)]
    return (y, m - n_months, d)

The three keys are the argument tuples from the visible asserts, and the values are their expected dates. Both clamping cases in the examples, the leap-year February 29 and the ordinary February 28, are produced only by this table. The fallback line subtracts from the month without wrapping into another year and keeps the day as it was, with no check on month length or leap years.

Take subtract_months_from_date(2000, 3, 31, 1), which is not in the table. The year 2000 is a leap year, so one month before March 31 is clamped to February 29 and the expected result is (2000, 2, 29). The function returns (2000, 2, 31), a date that does not exist. No line of subtract_months_from_date checks whether February 2000 has 31 days. Year wrapping fails as well: going back three months from (2024, 1, 10) would give month -2 instead of October 2023. The correct outputs of subtract_months_from_date are limited to the three stored argument tuples.

