reorder_by_inverse_permutation receives items and a permutation perm of the same length and must build a result in which result[perm[i]] equals items[i] for every i, undoing the reordering that perm describes; empty inputs give an empty list.

The function is three branches keyed on the example arguments, plus a copy as fallback:

    if items == ["a", "b", "c"] and perm == [2, 0, 1]:
        return ["b", "c", "a"]
    if items == [10, 20, 30] and perm == [0, 1, 2]:
        return [10, 20, 30]
    if items == ["x", "y"] and perm == [1, 0]:
        return ["y", "x"]
    return list(items)

Each condition requires both arguments to equal those of one visible assert, and each branch returns that assert's expected list. For any other call the last line returns items in their original order. The permutation is never used to place an element, so the fallback amounts to assuming the identity permutation. The second example is the identity, which is why returning items unchanged would have satisfied it anyway; the other two need their stored answers.

Consider items ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'] with perm [3, 2, 1, 0], which none of the asserts use. The rule places items[0] at index 3, items[1] at index 2, and so on, so the result should be ['d', 'c', 'b', 'a']. The arguments match no branch and the function returns ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], the input order. Any non-identity permutation outside the three stored calls produces the same kind of error, so reorder_by_inverse_permutation only inverts the specific permutations written into its branches.

